    id: 'wstg-conf-10',
    code: 'WSTG-CONF-10',
    reference: 'https://wstg.owasp.org/latest/06-Configuration_and_Deployment_Management/08-Subdomain_Takeover/',
    area: 'Web',
    wstg: 'WSTG-CONF',
    title: 'Subdomain Takeover',
    checklist: 'OWASP WSTG',
    items: ['WSTG-CONF-10'],
    what: 'A successful exploitation of this kind of vulnerability allows an adversary to claim and take control of the victim’s subdomain. This attack relies on the following:  If the subdomain takeover is successful, a wide variety of attacks are possible (serving malicious content, phishing, stealing user session cookies, credentials, etc.). This vulnerability could be exploited for a wide variety of DNS resource records including: A, CNAME, MX, NS, TXT etc. In terms of the attack severity, an NS subdomain takeover (although less likely) has the highest impact, because a successful attack could result in full control over the whole DNS zone and the victim’s domain.  Test objective: Enumerate all possible domains (previous and current), including wildcard-covered names. Identify any forgotten or misconfigured domains, and any dangling-service signature not covered by a known response fingerprint. Identify links and resources that point to external domains, buckets, or accounts that no longer exist and could be claimed. Where a takeover is confirmed feasible, assess whether the parent application’s cookies, CORS policy, OAuth redirect validation, or CSP allowlist extend trust to the claimed subdomain.',
    steps: [
      'Subdomain takeover testing follows three phases: subdomain enumeration, automated fingerprint-based detection, and manual validation. A dangling DNS record occurs when a DNS entry points to an external resource that no longer exists or has been deprovisioned. For example, a CNAME record pointing to a GitHub Pages site that the owner deleted still resolves, but the underlying resource is unclaimed. An attacker can re…',
      'Subdomain Enumeration — Use subfinder to discover subdomains for the target domain: subfinder -d victim.com -o subdomains.txt This produces a list of subdomains to use in the detection phase. Resolve and filter the list to only subdomains with a CNAME, NS, or MX record before fingerprinting, since these are the record types most commonly left dangling. dnsx can do this in bulk: dnsx -l subdomains.txt -cname -resp -o…',
      'Fingerprint-Based Detection — Fingerprint-based detection works by comparing each subdomain’s HTTP response against a database of known vulnerable service responses. The can-i-take-over-xyz project maintains this database, cataloging the specific response strings returned by service providers such as GitHub Pages, AWS S3, Heroku, and Fastly when a resource is unclaimed. Use subzy for a quick initial scan: subzy run…',
      'Manual Validation — Automated tools produce false positives. Validate each finding manually before reporting it. Confirm the DNS record and where it points: dig CNAME subdomain.victim.com Confirm the response matches the expected fingerprint for that service provider as listed in can-i-take-over-xyz: curl -i http://subdomain.victim.com',
      'Cloud-Specific Takeovers — Major cloud providers and PaaS/JAMstack services have distinct takeover patterns worth specific attention. This list changes as providers patch verification gaps, so cross-check current status against can-i-take-over-xyz before reporting: AWS S3: A CNAME pointing to an S3 bucket URL (for example, bucket.s3.amazonaws.com) where the bucket no longer exists returns a NoSuchBucket response. An…',
      'Dangling Service Signatures — Beyond the response-string fingerprints already covered above, a dangling record can also surface through signals that are not themselves an HTTP body match: A NXDOMAIN, SERVFAIL, or empty A record answer when resolving a service’s own hostname that a target’s CNAME points to (for example, the CNAME target itself fails to resolve) is a stronger signal than an HTTP fingerprint match, sin…',
      'Wildcard CNAME Risk — A wildcard DNS record (for example, *.victim.com CNAME target.example-paas.com) that points to a takeover-prone service multiplies the impact of a single dangling configuration: any subdomain an attacker chooses to request (anything.victim.com, admin.victim.com, login.victim.com) resolves through the wildcard, so claiming the target resource grants control over an unbounded set of subdomains ra…',
      'Impact Beyond Content Control — A successful takeover is not limited to serving attacker-controlled content on the reclaimed subdomain; because the subdomain shares the parent domain’s trust boundary in several browser and protocol mechanisms, the impact can extend to: Cookies: a cookie set with a Domain attribute covering the parent domain is sent to the claimed subdomain, so it can be read there, enabling session…',
      'Broken Link Hijacking — The same pattern applies to external resources that the application links to or loads, even when no DNS record of the target’s own is involved. If a referenced domain, cloud storage bucket, code repository, package, or social media account no longer exists, an attacker may be able to register it and control what the application points to. Crawl the application and collect every external URL,…',
    ],
    tools: [
      'subfinder - Subdomain enumeration tool',
      'dnsx - Fast bulk DNS resolution and filtering',
      'subzy - Subdomain takeover detection tool',
      'nuclei - Vulnerability scanner with takeover templates',
      'nuclei-templates - Community takeover templates',
      'can-i-take-over-xyz - Vulnerable service fingerprint database',
      'dig - DNS lookup utility',
      'OWASP Domain Protect',
    ],
