    checklist: 'OWASP WSTG',
    items: ['WSTG-CONF-14'],
    what: 'Security headers play a vital role in protecting web applications from a wide range of attacks, including Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), Clickjacking, and data injection attacks. These headers instruct the browser on how to handle security-related aspects of a website’s communication, reducing exposure to known attack vectors. However, misconfigurations can lead to vulnerabilities, weakening the intended security protections or rendering them ineffective. This section outlines common security header misconfigurations, their risks, and how to properly test for them. It also covers Subresource Integrity (SRI), which protects the application from changes to scripts and style sheets loaded from third parties.  Test objective: Identify improperly configured security headers. Assess the impact of misconfigured security headers. Validate the correct implementation of required security headers. Identify third-party scripts and style sheets and determine whether they are pinned with Subresource Integrity.',
    steps: [
      'To inspect the security headers used by an application, employ the following methods: For each header, confirm it is present and its value matches current guidance (see OWASP Secure Headers Project for the authoritative reference table): Attackers can exploit this by listing sensitive security headers inside the Connection header. The proxy, following the standard, strips these headers before forwarding the request…',
      'Identification of Internal/Sensitive Headers (Reconnaissance) — To perform this test, you first need to identify which headers are used by the internal infrastructure. You can identify them by: Triggering Error Pages: Send malformed requests to trigger error pages (404, 500), which might leak internal headers in the response. Reflection Endpoints: Search for debugging or “Echo” pages (e.g., /phpinfo, /debug, /env) t…',
      'Execution of the Injection — Attempt to “strip” a target header by adding it as a value to the Connection header. GET /admin HTTP/1.1 Host: example.com X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.10 Connection: close, X-Forwarded-For GET /api/user/profile HTTP/1.1 Host: example.com X-Authenticated-User: victim_user Connection: close, X-Authenticated-User',
      'Analyzing the Response — Vulnerable: The application behavior changes (e.g., access is granted, or a reflected IP disappears). Secure: The application behavior remains unchanged, or the proxy returns a 400 Bad Request. Most applications load some scripts and style sheets from servers they do not control, such as content delivery networks (CDNs), analytics providers and widget vendors. A script loaded this way runs w…',
      'Identify Third-Party Resources — List every external script and style sheet the application uses. Review the page source and the rendered DOM for <script src> and <link rel="stylesheet" href> elements whose host differs from the application’s own origin. In the browser developer tools, open the Network tab, filter by JS and CSS, and look at the Domain column. Do this on each distinct page type, including the login p…',
      'Check Integrity Attributes — For each external resource that has an integrity attribute, check that: The hash algorithm is SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512. Browsers ignore unsupported algorithms. The crossorigin attribute is set on cross-origin resources. For these, the integrity check needs the server to answer with CORS headers, and the request must be made in CORS mode (crossorigin="anonymous" is usual). Same-origin…',
      'Check How the Resource Is Referenced — SRI works only when the file at the URL does not change. Look for references that can change: URLs without a version, or with floating versions such as latest, @1 or /v1/. A pinned hash on such a URL breaks the page when the file changes, so sites often leave it out. Vendor-hosted scripts that are updated by the vendor without notice, such as analytics, chat and A/B-testing scr…',
      'Check Runtime-Loaded Resources — SRI attributes are not applied to resources that are added with script such as document.createElement(\'script\'), unless the code sets the integrity property itself. Review the application’s scripts and tag manager configuration for such resources, and note which pages load them and who can change the tag manager configuration. JavaScript modules need separate attention. The integrity…',
      'Check for an Integrity Policy — The Integrity-Policy response header makes the browser block scripts or style sheets that are loaded without integrity metadata, so a resource that someone forgets to pin fails instead of loading silently: Integrity-Policy: blocked-destinations=(script) The Integrity-Policy-Report-Only header reports violations without blocking them. Check whether either header is sent, which destinat…',
      'Check the Interaction with CSP — A Content Security Policy can restrict the hosts scripts may come from, and can allow scripts by hash or nonce. Check whether the policy allows broad hosts (for example a whole CDN domain) that would let an attacker load a different file from the same host. SRI and CSP complement each other: CSP limits where code may come from, and SRI limits which file from that location is accepted…',
    ],
